# privoxy user.action - the rules this machine adds on top of privoxy's own
# default.action. Managed by MacbookSetup (Ansible/roles/privoxy/files/), so
# edit it there and re-run the play rather than editing the copy in
# ~/.config/privoxy.
#
# Syntax: a {+action -action} header applies to every pattern under it, until
# the next header. Patterns are host[/path]; a leading dot matches subdomains.
# `privoxy --config-test` is not a thing - re-run the play, then check
# ~/Library/Logs/privoxy.log for "Invalid action section".

{{alias}}
# Shorthands, so the intent reads rather than the mechanism.
inspect    = +https-inspection
no-inspect = -https-inspection
deny       = +block{Blocked by privoxy user.action.}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Inspect TLS by default
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Without this privoxy only ever sees "CONNECT host:443" and can block whole
# hosts and nothing finer. Unlike the AdGuard Home setup this replaced,
# unbound does no content filtering of its own, so this is the only blocking
# this machine does at all. URL-path filtering on HTTPS is the entire reason
# the CA exists.
{inspect}
/

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Exceptions: everything that pins its certificates
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A pinned client does not care that the machine trusts the privoxy CA; it
# compares against the key it was shipped with, sees a different one, and
# fails - usually with a misleading error. These are the ones that reliably
# break; add to the list when something starts failing only while the proxy
# is in the path.
#
# The symptom to watch for in ~/Library/Logs/privoxy.log is a TLS handshake
# error against a host you did not expect to see there.
{no-inspect}
# The endpoint security agent. This one is not about breakage: intercepting
# and decrypting the traffic of the tool that watches this machine is wrong
# regardless of whether it would work, and it reports to someone else's
# console. Measured: it is one of the busiest destinations on this machine.
.sentinelone.net

.apple.com
.icloud.com
.mzstatic.com
.push.apple.com

# Microsoft is several domains, and the obvious one does not cover them.
# Measured the hard way: `.microsoft.com` does not match
# login.microsoftonline.com, which was being inspected while this list looked
# complete. teams.cloud.microsoft rejected the handshake outright.
.microsoft.com
.cloud.microsoft
.microsoftonline.com
.microsoftonline-p.com
.sharepoint.com
.office.com
.office365.com
.live.com
.windowsupdate.com

# Firefox/Zen phone home for captive-portal detection and updates through
# NSS, which only sees the CA once the enterprise-roots policy has been
# applied and the browser restarted.
.firefox.com
.mozilla.com
.mozilla.net

.dropbox.com
.spotify.com
.whatsapp.net
.signal.org
.tailscale.com
.tailscale.io
# Banking and anything with a hardware token belongs here too - decrypting it
# locally buys nothing and breaks apps that check for exactly this.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. URL blocking
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# privoxy's default.action already covers the usual ad/tracker surface; this
# section is for the paths *this* machine should not reach, which is a
# decision, not a default. Two worked examples:
#
#   {deny}
#   .example.com/ads/
#   .example.com/sponsored*
#
# and an exception carved back out of a block:
#
#   {-block}
#   .example.com/ads/thing-i-actually-want

# n8n (LocalKubernetes/roles/host_n8n) is reached via a schretzi.net-rooted
# alias so Gmail's OAuth2 redirect URI passes Google's public-suffix check;
# .kiac itself bypasses this proxy entirely (see NO_PROXY/proxy bypass
# domains), so kiac.schretzi.net is the only one that actually reaches here.
# Its Vite-bundled asset filenames (PopoverTrigger-*.js, PopperContent-*.js)
# false-positive default.action's generic ad-blocking path pattern
# `pop[-_]?(up|over|under|open)`, which was blocking the editor UI outright.
{-block}
.kiac.schretzi.net

# PyPI serves every artifact from a blake2-hash path,
# /packages/<2 hex>/<2 hex>/<64 hex>/<name>.whl. When the second pair happens
# to be the two letters "ad", default.action's generic ad-path pattern matches
# and privoxy returns 403 - so roughly one package in 256 fails to download,
# with no relation to which package it is. Measured: certifi is one of them,
# which is enough to break every `uv tool install` (see the uv_tools role).
# Same class of false positive as the kiac entry above.
.pythonhosted.org
